P:1 And it happened that, after Israel was driven into captivity, and Jerusalem was deserted,
P:3 And sighing with a bitter soul, and mourning, he said:
The Governess of the Gentiles has become like a widow.
There is no one to be a comfort to her and to all her beloved.
She has lived among the nations and not found rest.
All her gates are destroyed.
Her virgins are filthy.
For the Lord has spoken against her, because of the multitude of her iniquities.
Jerusalem has remembered the days of her affliction and the betrayal of all her desirable ones, whom she held from the days of antiquity, when her people fell into the hand of the enemy, and there was no one to be a helper.
Because of this, she has become unstable.
Then she groaned and turned away again.
Her filth is on her feet, and her end has not been remembered.
O Lord, look upon my affliction, for the adversary has been lifted up.
The enemy has sent his hand against all her desirable ones.
They have given up whatever was precious in exchange for food, so as to remain alive.
For he has made me a vintage, just as the Lord has spoken in the day of his furious anger.
From on high, he has sent fire into my bones, and he has educated me.
He has placed me in desolation, consumed by grief, all day long.
Vigilant is the yoke of my iniquities.
My virtue has been weakened.
He has called forth time against me, so as to crush my elect ones.
For the consoler has been far away from me, changing my soul.
The Lord has given orders against Jacob; his enemies are all around him.
I beg all people to listen and to see my sorrow.
My priests and my elders have been consumed in the city.
My bowels have been disturbed, my heart has been subverted within me, for I am filled with bitterness.
All my enemies have heard of my misfortune; they have rejoiced that you caused it.
And make vintage of them, just as you made vintage of me, because of all my iniquities.
O how the Lord has covered the daughter of Zion with gloom in his fury!
In his fury, he has destroyed the fortifications of the virgin of Judah, and he has thrown them down to the ground.
He has drawn back his right hand before the face of the enemy.
He has fixed his right hand like an adversary.
He has poured out his indignation like fire.
The Lord has become like an enemy.
He has thrown down all of his defenses.
And he has torn apart her tent like a garden.
In Zion, the Lord has delivered feast and Sabbath into oblivion, and king and priest into disgrace, and into the indignation of his fury.
The Lord has pushed away his own altar.
He has delivered the walls of its towers into the hand of the enemy.
He has ruined and crushed its bars.
There is no law, and her prophets have found no vision from the Lord.
The elders of the daughter of Zion have become idle; they sit on the ground.
To what shall I equate you, so as to console you, O virgin daughter of Zion?
Who will cure you?
Your prophets have seen false and foolish things for you.
All those who passed by the way have clapped their hands over you.
We have found it, we have seen it.”
The Lord has done what he decided to do.
Their heart cried out to the Lord from the walls of the daughter of Zion.
Pour out your heart like water before the sight of the Lord.
My virgins and my youths have fallen by the sword.
You have struck down, and you have not shown pity.
Those whom I educated and nourished, my enemy has consumed.
Yet even when I cry out and beg, he excludes my prayer.
He has enclosed my ways with square stones; he has subverted my paths.
He has become to me like a bear lying in ambush, like a lion in hiding.
He has subverted my paths, and he has broken me.
For his compassion has not passed away.
I know it at first light; great is your faithfulness.
“The Lord is my portion,” said my soul.
For he has lifted it upon himself.
He shall place his mouth in the dirt, if perhaps there may be hope.
He shall give his cheek to those who strike him; he shall be saturated with reproaches.
For the Lord will not rebuke forever.
For, if he has cast down, he will also have compassion, according to the multitude of his mercies.
as if to crush under his feet all the prisoners of the land,
as if to pervert a man in his judgment: the Lord does not do this.
Who is this, who said to do what the Lord did not command?
Why has a living man murmured, a man suffering for his sins?
Let us examine our ways, and seek out, and return to the Lord.
Let us lift up our hearts, with our hands, toward the Lord in the heavens.
You have killed, and have not spared.
You have set a cloud opposite you, lest our prayer pass through.
In the midst of the peoples, you have uprooted me and cast me out.
All our enemies have opened their mouths over us.
My eye has brought forth streams of water at the contrition of the daughter of my people.
My eye has been afflicted, and it has not been quieted, because there would be no rest
until the Lord looked down and saw from the heavens.
My eye has exhausted my soul over every one of the daughters of my city.
My enemies have chased me, and they have caught me like a bird, without reason.
My life has fallen into a pit, and they have placed a stone over me.
The waters have flooded over my head.
Do not turn away your ear from my sobbing and my cries.
You drew near in the daytime, when I called upon you.
You are the Redeemer of my life.
You have seen, O Lord, their iniquity against me.
O how the gold has become dulled, the finest color has been altered, the stones of the sanctuary have been scattered at the head of every street.
The famous sons of Zion, and those clothed with the foremost gold: how they have become like earthen vessels, the work of the hands of a potter.
Yet even savages expose their breast and give milk to their young.
The little ones have asked for bread, and there was no one to break it for them.
Those who were fed indulgently have passed away in the roads.
Her Nazirites were whiter than snow, shinier than milk, more ruddy than ancient ivory, more beautiful than sapphire.
The hands of pitiable women have boiled their sons.
The kings of the earth, and all the inhabitants of the world, did not believe that the adversary and the enemy would enter through the gates of Jerusalem.
It is because of the sins of her prophets, and the iniquities of her priests, who have shed the blood of the just in her midst.
They have wandered in the streets like the blind; they have been defiled with blood.
The face of the Lord has divided them.
While we were still standing, our eyes failed, expecting help for us in vain, when we looked attentively toward a nation that was not able to save.
Our footsteps have slipped on the paths of our own streets.
Our days have been completed, for our end has arrived.
Your iniquity has been completed, O daughter of Zion.
He has visited your iniquity, O daughter of Edom; he has uncovered your sins. Lamentations 5
Consider and look kindly upon our disgrace.
5:3 We have become orphans without a father; our mothers are like widows.
We acquired our wood for a price.
Being weary, no rest was given to us.
5:7 Our fathers have sinned, and are not.
5:8 Servants have become rulers over us.
5:9 We obtained our bread at the risk of our lives, before the face of the sword, in the wilderness.
5:11 They humiliated the women in Zion and the virgins in the cities of Judah.
