Impact Of Plastics: Scientists Found Micro Plastics In Human Blood For The First Time! News
Whether it’s from the deepest oceans to the tallest mountains, or from the air and soil to the food chain, microplastic debris is already present almost everywhere on the Earth. Now, more studies have proved that micro plastics have “invaded” human blood.
Usually, plastic debris less than 5mm in diameter are referred to”micro plastics”, and its very small volume makes it difficult for us to notice its existence.
The study examined blood samples from 22 anonymous healthy volunteers and found that 77% of the samples contained microplastics with an average concentration of 1.6 micrograms per milliliter.
PET is usually used for the appearance of mineral water bottles, beverage bottles and various household appliances.
What is even more astonishing is that researchers found up to three different types of micro plastics in a blood sample.
How does micro plastics enter the blood?
The researchers said there may be other types of microplastics in the blood, but they did not detect particles larger than the diameter of the sampling needle in this study.
Where is the way out for plastic pollution?
Establish the concept of environmental protection and strive to be the propagandist of plastic pollution control. Do not buy or use non-degradable plastic products, actively publicize the necessity and urgency of banning plastics to family and friends, advocate green and environmentally friendly production and lifestyles, and promote the formation of a good atmosphere in which individuals lead families and families to influence society, and all people participate in banning plastics.
Reject “white pollution” and strive to be a green ecological defender. Strengthen the sense of ownership, start from now, start from bit by bit, strictly abide by the “plastic ban”, make green environmental protection a new fashion, pick up vegetable baskets and paper bags.
