Guadalupe Ortiz, 47, faces felony charges of assault or assault and serious bodily injury by a public official in connection with the April 1 incident.
Officials said that when the deputies were unable to get the prisoners to comply, Ortiz and the other deputies offered to help.
More than six hours later, another deputy talked to the prisoner during the security check and requested medical treatment of the victim’s arm, which was described as red and peeling.
Officials said Ortiz served as deputy for 19 years and served as the sheriff’s special office before being fired last week.
Ortiz is scheduled to be summoned on January 11, 2022. If convicted, he will face up to four years in prison.
