In September 2016, Politikoffee surveyed its members to get a general idea of the eating habits of young Cambodians. The 38 respondents aged from 18 to 35, with most in their 20s.
In some cases, patterns emerge when answers are combined, such as where people eat. Home-cooked food bought from a traditional market (19) or a supermarket (15) both ranked highly, followed by street food (19) and air-conditioned restaurants (15), then traditional markets (10) and fast food (8).
The responses were more clear-cut about who is responsible for healthy food. Combined, those surveyed believe the duty lies with the government (32), themselves (29) and food companies (23).
Familiarity with viral bacterial parasitic contaminations (27) is higher than food safety (23) and nearly as high as healthy food (29). Responses about hygienic treatment of food and organic food were split more evenly.
Unlikely. Why improve when you win competitions and people think you’re good enough?
Whenever someone accomplishes anything slightly above average, they are heavily praised because they’re Cambodian. People who criticize would be dubbed as “haters”.
The world is a giant competition. Why compare ourselves with the mediocre?
shoot for the sky and you’ll land… not much higher than the ground. That would be good enough because at least it wasn’t on the ground, would it not?
One argument that highly hinders improvement is: “If you’re so great, why don’t you do it?” That’s absurd.
