Based on the DSM-5 definition, attention deficit hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) is a disorder characterized by attention deficit and manifestations of hyperactivity / impulsivity.
From this perspective, the score used to identify gifted individuals is, in most cases, an intelligence quotient (IQ) of at least 130. A problematic aspect that concerns people with the latter label is the possible presence of ADHD and which, at least according to some data, could occur significantly more frequently.
However, the manifestation of ADHD may differ between people with high IQ and those with an intellectual level within the normal range.
However, in some specific subdomains of ADHD, the gifted with attention deficit hyperactivity disorder show more marked characteristics; for example, compared to the lower modulation of motor activity and verbal activity, together with a lower tendency to reflect on questions. In conclusion...
Furthermore, the presence of surplus gift does not seem to have a predominant impact on the manifestation of ADHD symptoms.
