In adults with ADHD, deficits in executive functions can occur in everyday life with impulsive decisions, poor tolerance to frustration, difficulty in time management (for example, being late and having poor knowledge of time), ability to manage and self-motivate, poor ability to plan and organize one's activities .
Some examples of prospective memory could be remembering to go to the doctor's appointment at 16:00 or taking medicine before having breakfast (see also our article on prospective memory in multiple sclerosis rehabilitation of perspective memory ).
The hypothesis is that procrastination may have to do with the poor predisposition to be projected into the future ( orientation to the future ) and with difficulty imagining it ( episodic future thinking ). People who tend to procrastination would be more oriented towards the present and would have more difficulty imagining future scenarios .
the authors of the study they selected two groups of adult subjects, one consisting of 29 people with ADHD and another consisting of 29 people with typical development. All have undergone tests immediate and delayed episodic memory , homework of prospective memory in the laboratory and homework perspective memory in everyday life ; they also completed a questionnaire on tendency to procrastination in daily life and a questionnaire to investigate the tendency to be projected into the future .
Research data indicate several interesting results:
A correlation was found between ability to remember one's intentions in daily life e Deferred episodic memory test in the laboratory (consistent with the importance of a correct functioning of the episodic memory for the efficiency of the prospective memory).
People with ADHD have reported one tendency to procrastination far superior to those with typical development.
La tendency to procrastination was strongly correlated with the number of planned actions actually performed , with the severity of ADHD and orientation towards the future.
However, it must be taken into account that this is one correlational research and therefore it can only indicate possible relationships between variables; it is therefore necessary that further research is conducted which highlights the possible causal links between the variables (cognitive characteristics) considered in this research.
The nature of procrastination: A meta-analytic and theoretical review of quintessential self-regulatory failure. Psychological bulletin , 133 (1), 65.
