Feel the"Mathematical intelligence" immutable (rather than modifiable) would have involved, on the part of a professor, a more hasty judgment on the skills of a hypothetical student?
The teacher's idea about immutable mathematical skills (instead of modifiable) would have been perceived by the student, involving a demotivation in the study of mathematics by the same?
Subsequently each student was asked to imagine being a math professor, with the task of judge a student's performance with a low grade in the first math test of the year. The "professor" had the task of saying how much the low performance had depended on little mathematical intelligence e how little commitment.
At this point one might ask: do beliefs about the malleability of mathematical intelligence lead to changes in pedagogical practices?
95 university students were recruited and divided into two groups: the first group was made to read a "scientific" article which explained that the88% of math skills of a person depend on genetic factors, while the second group was made to read an article stating the opposite, namely that the88% of math skills depend on will of the subject to develop them (from his commitment). The subjects of both groups had, once again, imagine you are maths teachers.
The results indicated that those who had read thearticle on predetermined mathematical intelligence genetically tended to attribute poor results in the math task to poor capacity instead of having little commitment and, perhaps more interestingly, it tended to console the student, also referring to wanting to implement strategies aimed at reduce the commitment in this matter by the student (for example, fewer exercises).
Now let's move on to the student's point of view. What effect does this consoling communication of results have on students?
44 were recruited students from a private American university and they were asked to imagine having failed a math exam. At this point the subjects were divided into 3 groups:
The second group received one communication of the same results based on a change of strategies aimed at increasing performance (for example, "I know you are a talented student and I want to change your study strategy, perhaps together with a tutor. I will call you more often and give you more demanding tasks.
The results showed that the students in the "consolation" group they perceived by the hypothetical professor his idea of immutable mathematical intelligence, as well as low expectations and investment in them. The students themselves, receiving consoling and commitment reduction responses, reported feeling less motivated and less encouraged than those who received other feedback, also expecting to have poorer results at the end of the year.
Although the teachers' strategies aimed at consular e reduce the effort on the part of the student have the aim of reducing his frustrations, the result would seem to produce different motion effects: the students would perceive poor motivation to engage in mathematics and poor expectations towards them by the professors.
