In two of the searches just mentioned scholars have developed a diagnostic algorithm capable of discriminating accurately accurately, among people with MCI or Alzheimer's disease, subjects able to guide from those unable, and this was done by cross-referencing data from neuropsychological evaluation, clinical interview and test of drive with a simulator.
In the group with vascular dementia, the worst performances were found in neuropsychological tests and in clinical interviews.
People with frontotemporal dementia were those who described themselves as more capable of driving than those belonging to the other two groups (although only 33% of them were suitable).
On the other hand, this study also indicates that some of the people with frontotemporal dementia and Lewy body dementia may be able to drive adequately.
Collectively, these results indicate that probably the methods of assessing the skills necessary for safe driving can differ greatly depending on the syndrome presented by the subject and the etiology behind it. Taking as reference the investigation tools used in the research described in this article the authors describe the issues that could make the results less reliable:
The parameters used in the driving simulator may not be representative of all the critical situations that a person with dementia might face in real car traffic. Furthermore, these parameters could have opposite meanings based on the type of pathology considered (slow driving could indicate a problem in vascular dementia and, on the contrary, it could be a signal of preserved self-control in frontotemporal dementia).
Also, rather than just using the dichotomy suitable e not suitable in assessing driving ability, it may be more cautious to divide the results into 3 categories by adding that of the indeterminate, postponing the latter to subsequent investigations and thus decreasing the risk of false negatives and false positives.
Assessing fitness to drive in patients with different types of dementia. Alzheimer disease and associated disorders, 32(1), 70.
