Dementia: Risk factors and protective factors - Cognitive Training
Risk factors can be divided into editable (such as type of diet, smoking, alcohol intake, education level and physical activity) e not editable (for example sex, genetics and age) and this distinction is fundamental because it allows us to understand which strategies to put in place for reduce the possibility of having dementia or at least make it happen as late as possible . This is precisely the purpose of the review .
The authors have summarized the results of many researches by examining the effect of the following risk and protection factors : Educational level .
An innate factor affecting schooling is innate cognitive ability. The researchers report that in a longitudinal research the 11-year-old boys col QI higher were less likely to develop dementia after the age of 70 .
The relationship between depression and dementia remains complex today and it is not clear whether it is a risk factor or a manifestation of dementia in old age. What many studies report in any case is the increased probability of dementia in the presence of depression.
The relationship between alcohol and dementia has never been fully clarified. Although the cognitive risks dictated by a large chronic use of alcohol are known, the authors of the review report conflicting data about moderate consumption (a protection or risk factor?
Diet . In this publication several studies are cited that agree on the importance of a balanced diet (in particular the Mediterranean diet and that for controlling hypertension) as a protective factor with respect to cognitive deficits.
Taking medications . The relationship between statin intake and memory loss (or even as a protective factor for Alzheimer's disease) does not appear clear; contradictory data also emerge regarding the relationship between the use of proton pump inhibitors and the higher incidence of Alzheimer's disease; a high cumulative use over the years of anticholinergic drugs would seem to increase the risk of dementia .
Apneas in the sonno . Obstructive sleep apneas result in relation to different declines in neurocognitive performance and their treatment seems to bring benefits in a relatively short time (after 3 months there is an increase in hippocampal volume and prefrontal structures) improving executive functions, memory, global attention and cognitive functioning.
Use of over-the-counter drugs . Even in this case there would not seem to be sufficient evidence to indicate the use of over-the-counter drugs as a factor protecting against cognitive decline.
Physical exercise . Although it has already been established that exercise is healthy for the brain, insufficient evidence has yet been produced to claim that physical activity helps prevent dementia (due to the methodological limitations of many publications).
Dementia: what it is and what can be done
