According to research presented in 2012, motivation to learn could contribute more than the IQ to achieving positive results in mathematics.
Specifically, it is compared the contribution of QI and that of motivation to learn with respect to results in mathematics, both immediate and long term. In particular, around 3500 children of an age corresponding roughly to that expected for lower secondary school students were subjected to this evaluation.
As easily expected, in the first phase of learning the results at the intellectual tests explained those at the mathematical tests (that is, at a QI higher corresponds to better mathematical learning);
So it could be summarized, at least according to what is reported in this publication, that the intelligence it would seem to make its contribution only (or more) in the initial approach to the new subject, gradually giving way to other factors (such as the interest and the perceived usefulness of the study topic) that reveal themselves in the subsequent learning phases , far more decisive.
