It is common experience the difficulty of fixing in the memory boring arguments and therefore the ease with which they "evaporate" from our mind. It is also quite well known how, on the contrary, topics that are interesting to us (both positive and negative) remain firmly in our heads.
The results of recent research by the University of California suggest an important starting point for reflection: being curious about something could predispose you to memorize not only the information that we consider relevant but also that of little interest.
Predictably, the people undergoing the experiment were able to remember the information much more which they found interesting compared to the others but the most surprising thing was that even the faces presented in the vicinity of these relevant notions were remembered more than those shown when subjectively irrelevant information was provided.
It must be specified that these are preliminary studies that must be taken with caution pending further confirmations and subsequent research that clarify the mechanisms underlying what has just been described. Research such as these, however, gives a good idea of how teaching methodologies can progress rapidly if they integrate with knowledge of psychology and cognitive neuroscience.
