Those who follow this site are now used to it, we often talk about ADHD. We talked about it from the point of view of those who live with children and young people with these characteristics, just as we talked about it from the point of view of specialists, especially regarding the neuropsychological profile.
From this point of view, scientific research is very useful, especially those that analyze the evolutionary trajectories of children over a long period of time. It must also be said that these studies, given their complexity, are really scarce; this time, however, we are lucky because we have found one, very interesting, which concerns the economic income of adults diagnosed with ADHD in developmental age.
People with ADHD had a percentage of unemployment higher than the normotypics, that is 22% against 13%.
The group of subjects diagnosed with ADHD He earned 37% less than the control group.
22% of people with ADHD received financial help from parents while in normotypics only 8%.
Finally, individuals with ADHD had asked economic emergency aid with twice the frequency compared to others.
In other words, the higher the level of education, the less economic discrepancy was observed compared to expectations in the "normal" population. However, it should be noted that the effect was only mitigated but did not disappear, that is, even in the presence of a high academic level, economic differences tended to persist, albeit to a lesser extent.
Compared to live with your parentsduring this time, people with ADHD went from 40% to 33% while the change was much more marked in the normotypics that went from 28% to 12%.
Individuals with ADHD, over 5 years, savings increased by $ 1.508 while normotypes increased savings by $ 3.722.
