A recent study by Fazio and colleagues (2020) have tried to answer the following questions:
What are the cognitive mechanisms that influence the learning of written English?
The study, carried out on a sample of 90 dyslexic children (compared with 90 normolectors), reported the following results:
La working memory affects the accuracy in reading words and non-words, while the phonological competence influences both the accuracy in reading words and non-words and the ability to perform dictation tasks. In this sense, phonological memory and phonological competence in general significantly influence the accuracy in the performance in reading and writing of English.
In conclusion, for the Italian students examined, both neuropsychological aspects (such as verbal working memory and phonological competence) and socio-economic aspects support the process of learning a second language.
